{"step_index":82,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-16T16:41:18Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-16T18:41:18+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-10-16T18:41:19+02:00\n\nThe command exited with code 0.\nOutput:\nsudo incus config set network.ovn.northbound_connection tcp:127.0.0.1:6641\r\n\r\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n# 3. Incus Initialization, ZFS Pool, and Underlay Bridge (incusbr0)\r\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\necho \"[3/8] Initializing Incus and configuring storage pool...\"\r\nif ! sudo incus storage show blueagent-zfs &>/dev/null; then\r\n    sudo incus storage create blueagent-zfs zfs\r\nfi\r\n\r\nsudo incus admin init --auto\r\n\r\necho \"[+] Setting up incusbr0 with DHCP and OVN IP pools...\"\r\nsudo incus network set incusbr0 ipv4.address=172.16.0.1/24 ipv4.nat=true\r\nsudo incus network set incusbr0 ipv4.dhcp.ranges=172.16.0.2-172.16.0.99 ipv4.ovn.ranges=172.16.0.100-172.16.0.199\r\n\r\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n# 4. Team 0 Production L2 Bridge\r\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\necho \"[4/8] Creating Team 0 production L2 bridge (br-team-0)...\"\r\nif ! ip link show dev br-team-0 &>/dev/null; then\r\n    sudo ip link add name br-team-0 type bridge\r\n    sudo ip addr add 10.0.1.1/24 dev br-team-0\r\n    sudo ip link set dev br-team-0 up\r\nfi\r\n\r\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n# 5. WireGuard L3 Point-to-Point Tunnel (wg-0)\r\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\necho \"[5/8] Generating keys and establishing WireGuard interface (wg-0)...\"\r\nwg genkey | tee wg-0-private.key | wg pubkey > wg-0-public.key\r\nwg genkey | tee client-0-private.key | wg pubkey > client-0-public.key\r\n\r\nif ! ip link show dev wg-0 &>/dev/null; then\r\n    sudo ip link add dev wg-0 type wireguard\r\n    sudo ip addr add 10.0.0.1/30 dev wg-0\r\n    sudo wg set wg-0 listen-port 51820 private-key wg-0-private.key\r\n    sudo wg set wg-0 peer \"$(cat client-0-public.key)\" allowed-ips 10.0.0.2/32\r\n    sudo ip link set dev wg-0 up\r\nfi\r\n\r\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n# 6. nftables Firewall: Forwarding, TCP MSS Clamping & NAT Masquerade\r\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\necho \"[6/8] Configuring nftables filtering and traffic engineering...\"\r\nsudo nft flush table inet blueagent 2>/dev/null || true\r\nsudo nft add table inet blueagent\r\nsudo nft add chain inet blueagent forward '{ type filter hook forward priority filter; policy accept; }'\r\nsudo nft add rule inet blueagent forward tcp flags syn / syn,rst tcp option maxseg size set rt mtu\r\nsudo nft add rule inet blueagent forward iifname \"br-team-0\" accept\r\nsudo nft add rule inet blueagent forward oifname \"br-team-0\" accept\r\nsudo nft add rule inet blueagent forward iifname \"wg-0\" accept\r\nsudo nft add rule inet blueagent forward oifname \"wg-0\" accept\r\nsudo nft add chain inet blueagent postrouting '{ type nat hook postrouting priority srcnat; policy accept; }'\r\nsudo nft add rule inet blueagent postrouting ip saddr 10.0.1.0/24 ip daddr != 10.0.1.0/24 masquerade\r\nsudo nft add rule inet blueagent postrouting ip saddr 10.0.0.0/30 ip daddr != 10.0.0.0/30 masquerade\r\n\r\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n# 7. Team 0 Profile, Container Launch & systemd-networkd Static IP\r\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\necho \"[7/8] Launching production container (c0-web) and configuring networking...\"\r\nsudo incus profile create team-0-profile 2>/dev/null || true\r\nsudo incus profile device add team-0-profile eth0 nic nictype=bridged parent=br-team-0 name=eth0 2>/dev/null || true\r\n\r\nif ! sudo incus info c0-web &>/dev/null; then\r\n    sudo incus launch ubuntu:22.04 c0-web --profile default --profile team-0-profile --storage blueagent-zfs\r\nfi\r\n\r\n# Inject static IP configuration via systemd-networkd (independent of netplan/cloud-init)\r\nsudo incus exec c0-web -- bash -c 'cat << \"EOF\" > /etc/systemd/network/10-eth0.network\r\n\n"}
{"step_index":83,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-16T16:41:19Z","input_tokens":3456,"cache_read_tokens":57410,"output_tokens":2519,"content":"### Part 1: Are the parameters feasible for your virtual machine?\n\n**Short answer:** **Yes for the lower and mid tiers, but conditionally feasible for the top tier ($m=15, n=10$)** depending on the container image and storage pool size.\n\nHere is the exact mathematical breakdown against your VM hardware (**AMD Ryzen 7 7700X 8-Core, 15 GiB RAM, 2 GiB Swap, 1.88 GiB ZFS**):\n\n| Tier $(m, n)$ | Live Containers | Peak Containers during Verify $(2 \\times m \\times n + m)$ | RAM Footprint (Alpine: ~25MB/ct) | RAM Footprint (Ubuntu: ~100MB/ct) | Feasibility on 15 GiB VM |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **$(2, 2)$** | 4 | **8 – 10** | ~0.25 GiB | ~1.0 GiB | **100% Feasible** (Instant) |\n| **$(5, 5)$** | 25 | **50 – 55** | ~1.4 GiB | ~5.5 GiB | **100% Feasible** (Safe) |\n| **$(10, 7)$** | 70 | **140 – 150** | ~3.75 GiB | ~15.0 GiB | **Feasible on Alpine** / Borderline OOM on Ubuntu |\n| **$(15, 10)$** | 150 | **300 – 315** | **~7.8 GiB** | **~31.5 GiB** | **Feasible ONLY with Alpine + ZFS expansion** |\n\n#### The Three Bottlenecks to Watch:\n\n1. **RAM (The Critical Threshold):**\n   * If we use **Ubuntu / systemd**: 315 containers will require $\\approx 31\\text{ GiB}$ of RAM. Your VM has 15 GiB, so Linux will trigger the OOM killer and freeze the machine.\n   * If we use **Alpine 3.20 (OpenRC/musl)**: 315 containers idle at $\\approx 20\\text{–}30\\text{ MB}$ each ($\\approx 7.8\\text{ GiB}$ total), which **fits comfortably inside your 15 GiB RAM**.\n2. **Storage (`blueagent-zfs` pool):**\n   * Currently, `blueagent-zfs.img` is only **1.88 GB** (`sudo zpool list`). Even with ZFS Copy-on-Write, 315 container metadata files and `/var/log` mounts will exhaust 1.88 GB.\n   * *Fix:* Your `/var` has 12 GB free and `/` has 29 GB free. We can easily resize `blueagent-zfs.img` to 8–10 GB.\n3. **CPU & Kernel Limits:**\n   * Your Ryzen 7 7700X (8 cores / 16 threads) can easily handle the scheduling.\n   * Kernel limits on your VM (`pid_max = 4,194,304`, `file-max = 9.22e18`) are already high enough.\n\n---\n\n### Part 2: Questions Before Implementing the Test Plan\n\nTo tailor the benchmarking suite for your thesis, please clarify these 4 points:\n\n1. **Container Base Image:**\n   * Shall we use **Alpine 3.20** (located in `/home/debian/bluectfd/yaml/alpine-3.20.yaml`) so we can safely scale all the way up to $m=15, n=10$ without hitting RAM limits?\n2. **ZFS Storage Pool Expansion:**\n   * Do you grant permission to expand the `blueagent-zfs.img` loopback image to **8–10 GB** (using the free space on `/var`) to prevent `ENOSPC` (disk full) crashes during high-concurrency tiers?\n3. **Verification Mode (Black-box vs. Agentless):**\n   * Do you want the verification tests to run:\n     * **Option A (Agentless):** Probes executed directly inside the cloned containers via `incus exec` (`target: \"container\"`).\n     * **Option B (Black-Box):** Spawning a dedicated `verify-runner` container per team that attacks the clones across the dark OVN switch (`target: \"verifier\"`).\n     * **Option C (Both):** Benchmark Option A across all tiers, and test Option B on lower tiers to show the architectural overhead delta in your thesis.\n4. **Execution Lifecycle per Grid Point:**\n   * For each $(m, n)$ point in the $4 \\times 4 = 16$ combinations, is it acceptable to follow a **clean-room cycle**:\n     $$\\text{Provision } m \\text{ teams} \\to \\text{Deploy } m \\times n \\text{ containers} \\to \\text{Run Concurrent Verifications} \\to \\text{Record Timings} \\to \\text{Clean Teardown}$$\n     before progressing to the next tier? (This prevents resource leakage across test runs).","thinking":"Initial assessment requires understanding the computational demands of the parameters $m$ and $n$ regarding team size. The query implicitly seeks feasibility within the virtual machine's resource constraints; therefore, the analysis must evaluate the potential workload generated by each parameter set. This evaluation will determine whether the given virtual machine resources can handle the implied computations.\n\n\nAnalyzing container resource requirements based on calculated concurrent counts for each tier reveals RAM as the primary constraint. Tier 4, with 315 concurrent containers, presents the most significant challenge. Initial estimates indicate the host's 15 GiB RAM may be insufficient when considering typical memory usage per container, especially with a standard distribution. Therefore, resource optimization, possibly through lightweight images or memory limits, is necessary for higher tiers.\n\n\nAnalysis suggests that lightweight base images or memory limits are critical for handling the maximum container count within the available RAM. Resizing the disk image or relocating it to a larger partition provides sufficient storage for container data.\n\nCrucially, before finalizing the implementation plan, user input is required regarding container base images and the verification target mode. The choice between an Alpine image and a full distribution, and also between in-container and black-box verification dramatically affects resource needs and test fidelity.\n\n\n"}
